<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Solution-wide build settings. MSBuild imports this automatically for every
  project beneath it, so a setting that belongs to the whole tree lives here
  rather than being copy-pasted into six .vcxproj files and drifting.

  SupportJustMyCode
    OFF. /JMC injects a call to __CheckForDebuggerJustMyCode at the top of
    EVERY function so the debugger can step over frames that are not yours.
    That is a fine trade in most codebases and a poor one here: the 6502
    inner loop is a chain of tiny functions (StepOne, FetchOperand, ReadByte,
    FindDevice, ResolveByte, vector::operator[]), several of them per
    emulated cycle, and each one now pays a probe.

    Profiling one Debug run of DOS33_SAVE_RoundTripsToDsk put
    __CheckForDebuggerJustMyCode at 24.5% of all samples, the single largest
    leaf in the process, ahead of Cpu::StepOne itself at 9.1%.

    Turning it off costs Just My Code stepping in the debugger: call stacks
    show library frames, and F10 steps into them. It changes nothing else.
    No optimization, no iterator debugging, no _DEBUG behavior, no asserts.
    Release never sets /JMC anyway, so scoping this to Debug would only add
    noise.

    It has to be ClCompile METADATA, not just the property of the same name:
    the property alone left /JMC on all 26 command lines. And it has to be
    here rather than in Directory.Build.targets, because an ItemDefinitionGroup
    only reaches items declared after it, and ClCompile items are declared by
    the .vcxproj itself. This file is imported first; that one is imported
    last.
-->
<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <SupportJustMyCode>false</SupportJustMyCode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    MultiProcessorCompilation
      ON. Without /MP, cl.exe compiles the files on its command line one at a
      time, on one core. Every project here was built that way, so an 8-thread
      machine ran a full build on one thread and left seven idle.

      It costs the most where it hurts most. A code-analysis build runs
      /analyze and the Core Check plugin as extra per-file passes, so each
      translation unit costs several times a plain compile, and none of it
      overlapped: the four analysis configurations took 17 to 22 minutes
      each.

      /MP is incompatible with /Gm (minimal rebuild), which the projects
      already disable, and with /Yc when one command line creates a
      precompiled header from several sources. MSBuild compiles the PCH
      source in its own cl.exe invocation, so that case does not arise.

      Build.ps1 also passes -m, which is the other half: /MP parallelizes the
      files within one project, -m parallelizes projects that do not depend
      on each other.
  -->

  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <SupportJustMyCode>false</SupportJustMyCode>
      <MultiProcessorCompilation>true</MultiProcessorCompilation>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>

  <!--
    Debug STL: bounds checks WITHOUT the iterator-tracking machinery.

    _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL defaults to 2 whenever _DEBUG is defined. Level 2
    bounds-checks container access AND makes every iterator carry a
    back-pointer to its container while every container keeps a linked list of
    its live iterators, so the STL can detect use-after-invalidation. The
    bookkeeping is the expensive half: the 6502 memory bus reads through
    std::vector, so the emulator pays it several times per emulated
    instruction.

    Measured on a pure-interpreter probe (min of 3 runs, full rebuild each):

      _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL=2 (default)   11.87 s
      =1                                  10.17 s   -14%
      =0                                   8.21 s   -31%
      =0 + _MSVC_STL_HARDENING=1           7.73 s   -35%
      Release floor                        2.52 s

    A flat =0 would be the wrong trade: it drops out-of-range detection on
    vector::operator[], which in an emulator indexing memory arrays on every
    instruction turns a precise assert into silent corruption. But the
    subscript check is guarded by

        #if _MSVC_STL_HARDENING_VECTOR || _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL != 0

    and _MSVC_STL_HARDENING_VECTOR derives from _MSVC_STL_HARDENING, which is
    an INDEPENDENT switch defaulting to 0. So =0 plus hardening keeps the
    check and drops only the bookkeeping. =1 does not: it still enables the
    checked-iterator machinery, which is why it recovers less than half as
    much.

    Scoped to Debug deliberately. This file applies to every configuration,
    and defining _MSVC_STL_HARDENING globally would add bounds checks to
    shipping Release code at an unmeasured cost.

    _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL is an ABI switch: container layouts change, so
    every translation unit and linked library must agree. Setting it here is
    what makes that true by construction, and MSVC's detect_mismatch pragma
    turns any disagreement into a link error rather than a crash.

    /RTC1 stays ON. It is another 22% of the Debug/Release gap, but it buys
    local-buffer-overrun and uninitialized-read detection, and the emulator
    writes through raw memory buffers, precisely what /RTCs protects.
  -->
  <ItemDefinitionGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'">
    <ClCompile>
      <PreprocessorDefinitions>_ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL=0;_MSVC_STL_HARDENING=1;%(PreprocessorDefinitions)</PreprocessorDefinitions>
    </ClCompile>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>

</Project>
